echo '<hr style="margin:28px 0;border:none;border-top:2px solid #e5e7eb;">';
echo '<h2>What This Philosophy Achieves</h2>';

echo '<p>The Igbo philosophy of the person is not a primitive animism or a simple folk belief. It is a coherent, internally consistent metaphysics of personal identity that answers, in its own terms, the hardest questions about what a person is:</p>';
echo '<ul>';
echo '<li><strong>What makes me <em>me</em>?</strong> — The answer is chi + onwe + gender + lineage: the spiritual covenant before birth, the self that acts within it, the gender that is essential to the soul, and the family into which the soul returns. These four constitute identity across time.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>Why are people different?</strong> — Chi. The metaphysical ground of individual difference is the pre-natal covenant — the quality and terms of the chi each soul carries into life. This is not fatalism: the onwe acts within the chi\'s field. But it explains why people born into similar circumstances have radically different lives.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>What happens at death?</strong> — The body returns to Ala; the mmụọ transitions to the spirit world; the soul becomes an ndichie (ancestor) who watches over the family. In time, the soul returns through ilo uwa — within the same family, in the same gender, recognized by the family it loves.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>What goes wrong?</strong> — Ọgbanje: the soul that failed to fully commit to the living world before birth, caught between worlds, cycling in rapid death and return. The cure is not spiritual but physical: find the iyi-uwa, break the covenant, free the soul.</li>';
echo '<li><strong>What is the good life?</strong> — A life lived with ọgụ (clean hands, moral integrity), in right relationship with chi, community, and ancestors, completed in full before returning. Not liberation from the world, but full inhabitation of it.</li>';
echo '</ul>';

echo '<p style="font-style:italic;margin-top:20px;padding:16px;border-left:3px solid #2d6a1f;background:rgba(45,106,31,.05);">"Onye kwe, chi ya ekwe." When a person agrees — truly commits, truly strives, truly inhabits their life — their chi agrees. The Igbo philosophy of the person places the living human being at the centre of a web of spiritual relationships and insists that within that web, human agency is real, human choices matter, and the quality of a life is determined not by fate alone but by the dialogue between the soul and its divine companion.</p>';

